=== B. fractal towers at prime powers, digit conservation, prime atoms ===
PASS squares mod 3^m (m = 2..5): every 3^j-coset partition is a module partition and level j is C3 (j even) / empty (j odd)
PASS cubes mod 3^m (m = 2..6): the 3^j-coset partition is a module partition iff j != 1 (mod 3): two-digit C9 blocks then one empty digit
PASS cube clock mod 9 = 9-cycle with loops (D = {0, 1, 8}), a prime digraph
PASS x^2 mod 9 has period 9, x^3 mod 9 has period 3 (the owner's two cycles)
PASS unit squares mod 3^m = {1 mod 3} (decided mod 3); unit cubes mod 3^m = {+-1 mod 9} (decided mod 9), m = 2..9
   conservation: squaring loses no 3-adic input digit and its image is read mod 3; cubing loses one input digit and its image needs one extra digit (mod 9)
PASS prime-level power clocks Cay(Z/p, D_k(p)) are prime (indecomposable) digraphs unless complete (3 <= p < 200, k = 2..8)
PASS every circulant Cay(Z/p, D) with p an odd prime is a prime digraph iff D minus {0} is neither empty nor everything (3 <= p < 60, 25 random D each)
PASS FINITE-EXACT: a power clock on n with >= 2 distinct primes is prime iff no prime-power factor has a clique module (1100 cases, n <= 300, k = 2..6)
PASS odd p: Cl(p^e, D_k(p^e)) has a clique module iff k | e-1 and gcd(k, p-1) = 1 (prime powers <= 300, k = 2..6); for e = 1 it is the whole complete clock
PASS tower law (p = 3,5,7,11; k = 2..6; p^m <= 2500): the type of d = p^v w depends only on v and w mod p^min(m-v, 1+v_p(k)), and is missing unless k | v
PASS squares mod 63, 45, 225 and cubes mod 175, 189, 225 are prime, although each has a tower factor (squares mod 9 and 25, cubes mod 25 and 27): CRT dissolves towers; cubes mod 9 is not a tower (it is the prime 9-cycle)
PASS digit conservation (Prop 6): on units mod p^m, x^k depends exactly on x mod p^(m-a), a = v_p(k), when m >= a+2; for m <= a+1 only on x mod p (on nothing when (p-1) | k) (p = 3, 5, 7, several k, p^m <= 20000)
=== C. Collatz on the mod-9 clocks ===
PASS odd n < 2e6: 3n+1 is a unit square mod 9 (digital root 1,4,7); T(n)^3 = (-1)^v (mod 9); T(n) is a square mod 9 iff v is even; exactly T(n) = (-1)^v * 4^(n+v) (mod 9): sign in the cube clock {1,8}, rotation n+v mod 3 in the square clock {1,4,7}
PASS forward Syracuse images mod 9: 1/9 on each square, 2/9 on each non-square (observed {1: 0.1111, 2: 0.2222, 4: 0.1111, 5: 0.2222, 7: 0.1111, 8: 0.2222})
PASS orbit law mod 9 (exact, two independent geometric halving counts): (1,2,4,5,7,8) get (8,16,11,4,2,22)/63
PASS second Syracuse iterates of odd n < 2e6 follow the orbit law to 1e-3 (observed x63: {1: 8.002, 2: 16.0, 4: 10.999, 5: 4.0, 7: 2.0, 8: 21.999}): 7 mod 9 is rare, 8 mod 9 common
